  "dom_id": "step-q11819",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041773/assets/7129baa9dcd41738dbdf04a022b8bbe6a864006896cc76cbee3594ff0a744608.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?",
  "worked_solution": "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(0,-2).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+0)^{2} & =4p(y-(-2)) \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4p(y+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(8,6),$ so we can substitute $x=8$ and $y=6$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4p(y+2) \\\\ {8}^{2} & =4p(6+2) \\\\ 64 & =32p \\\\ p & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4p(y+2) \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4(2)(y+2) \\\\ {x}^{2} & =8(y+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
